---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';

const services = [
  { name: 'Vaultwarden', desc: 'Password vault', port: ':8081' },
  { name: 'Gitea', desc: 'Self-hosted git + CI', port: ':8087' },
  { name: 'Headscale', desc: 'Self-hosted mesh control plane', port: ':8086' },
  { name: 'Pi-hole', desc: 'DNS ad-blocking', port: ':8053' },
  { name: 'Samba + Syncthing', desc: 'Storage + sync', port: ':445/:22000' },
  { name: 'Uptime Kuma', desc: 'Service monitoring', port: ':8082' },
  { name: 'Netdata', desc: 'Real-time metrics', port: ':19999' },
];
---

<Base title="Homelab Case Study">
  <p class="mono-chip inline-block mb-5">case-study/homelab &middot; 84 commits &middot; 1 raspberry pi</p>
  <h1 class="font-display text-4xl md:text-5xl font-bold mb-6 text-text leading-tight">
    I built a whole datacenter on a <span class="grad-text">$35 board</span>.<br />Then I deleted most of it.
  </h1>
  <p class="text-lg text-text-muted max-w-2xl mb-16">
    This is the honest version &mdash; not the "look how many containers I run" flex.
    It's a story about wanting control, over-building to get it, getting humbled by an outage,
    and learning that the senior move is subtraction.
  </p>

  <!-- Stat band -->
  <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-6 mb-20 reveal">
    <div class="border border-surface-light rounded-2xl px-6 py-4 bg-surface-light/20">
      <div class="font-display text-4xl font-bold grad-text">30&rarr;9</div>
      <div class="mono-chip inline-block mt-2">containers: peak vs. live</div>
    </div>
    <div class="border border-surface-light rounded-2xl px-6 py-4 bg-surface-light/20">
      <div class="font-display text-4xl font-bold grad-text">2&rarr;1</div>
      <div class="mono-chip inline-block mt-2">nodes: mesh vs. one pi</div>
    </div>
    <div class="border border-surface-light rounded-2xl px-6 py-4 bg-surface-light/20">
      <div class="font-display text-4xl font-bold grad-text">6&rarr;1</div>
      <div class="mono-chip inline-block mt-2">metrics stack &rarr; netdata</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- THE STORY -->
  <section class="mb-20 max-w-2xl">
    <div class="reveal mb-12">
      <p class="mono-chip inline-block mb-3">00 — the itch</p>
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold mb-3">I didn't want to rent my own life.</h2>
      <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
        My passwords lived on someone else's server. My files, my photos, my DNS &mdash; all rented from
        companies that could rate-limit me, price-hike me, or lock me out on a whim. I wanted the boring
        stuff to be <em>mine</em>. A Raspberry Pi 4B costs about $35 and never sleeps, so that's where it started.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="reveal mb-12">
      <p class="mono-chip inline-block mb-3">01 — naive ambition</p>
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold mb-3">Then I tried to be "a real engineer."</h2>
      <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-3">
        I threw everything onto the Pi, then got greedy and reached for Kubernetes &mdash; K3s, ArgoCD,
        Helmfile &mdash; because that's what "serious" infra looks like on paper. I watched the control
        plane alone eat 600&ndash;800MB of a 4GB box doing absolutely nothing useful.
      </p>
      <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
        So I ripped it all out. <span class="text-accent font-medium">Lesson one: don't cargo-cult tools
        you don't need.</span> Docker Compose did everything I actually required, on a fraction of the RAM.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="reveal mb-12">
      <p class="mono-chip inline-block mb-3">02 — the empire phase</p>
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold mb-3">I built an ops team for one person.</h2>
      <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
        Added a second machine. Stood up a <em>full</em> observability + auth + secrets stack &mdash;
        Grafana, Prometheus, Loki, Tempo, Alertmanager, Authelia, Infisical. Thirty containers across two
        nodes. Dashboards everywhere. It felt like running a real platform. It was also wildly
        over-engineered for a homelab with exactly one user, and somewhere in the back of my head I knew it.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="reveal mb-12">
      <p class="mono-chip inline-block mb-3">03 — the outage that changed everything</p>
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold mb-3">Then I got locked out of my own vault.</h2>
      <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-3">
        The whole point of self-hosting was <em>not</em> depending on someone else. But my mesh network
        still ran through a third-party coordination server &mdash; and one day it went down and took my
        access to my own password vault with it. The exact failure I'd been trying to avoid.
      </p>
      <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
        So I replaced the last piece I didn't control: I self-hosted the mesh control plane itself
        (Headscale). <span class="text-accent font-medium">Lesson two: a dependency you can't see is still
        a dependency.</span>
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="reveal mb-4">
      <p class="mono-chip inline-block mb-3">04 — the maturity move</p>
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold mb-3">I asked which of these I actually open.</h2>
      <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-3">
        Thirty containers. I sat down and asked an uncomfortable question: which of these do I genuinely
        use? The answer was nine. So I retired the other twenty-one &mdash; the entire observability empire,
        the auth layer, the reverse proxy &mdash; and replaced a six-container metrics stack with one
        (Netdata).
      </p>
      <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed">
        I didn't <code class="font-mono-accent text-accent">rm -rf</code> it. Everything decommissioned
        moved through a holding folder, documented in a decision record, recoverable via git history.
        <span class="text-accent font-medium">Lesson three: the flex isn't the build &mdash; it's the
        restraint.</span> Juniors add. Seniors remove.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Receipts: real live numbers pulled from the running dashboards -->
  <section class="mb-20 reveal">
    <h2 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold mb-2">The <span class="grad-text">receipts</span></h2>
    <p class="text-text-muted mb-6">Not a mockup. Live numbers from the box, right now.</p>
    <div class="terminal text-sm">
      <div class="terminal-bar">
        <span class="terminal-dot" style="background:#ff5f56"></span>
        <span class="terminal-dot" style="background:#ffbd2e"></span>
        <span class="terminal-dot" style="background:#27c93f"></span>
        <span class="ml-2 text-xs text-text-muted">vansh@autobot: ~ &mdash; status</span>
      </div>
      <div class="p-5 leading-relaxed">
        <p><span class="text-accent">$</span> uptime-kuma --summary</p>
        <p class="text-text-muted mb-3">
          10 / 10 monitors <span style="color:#22c55e">UP</span> &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; 98&ndash;100% uptime across the board
        </p>
        <p><span class="text-accent">$</span> netdata --collected</p>
        <p class="text-text-muted mb-3">5,095 metrics live &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; 1 node &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; 14d high-res retention</p>
        <p><span class="text-accent">$</span> uname -a &amp;&amp; nproc &amp;&amp; free -h</p>
        <p class="text-text-muted">
          Debian aarch64 (kernel 6.18) &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; 4 cores @ 1.50GHz &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; 3.71GB RAM &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; 1.86TB SSD
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="text-xs text-text-muted mt-3">
      Every service self-hosted on one Raspberry Pi 4B, monitored by itself, reachable only over an encrypted mesh.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Live services -->
  <section class="mb-20 reveal">
    <h2 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold mb-2">What's <span class="grad-text">actually</span> running</h2>
    <p class="text-text-muted mb-6">Nine services. Each at its own address, over an encrypted mesh &mdash; no reverse proxy, no single point of failure.</p>
    <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-4">
      {services.map((svc) => (
        <div class="card-lift border border-surface-light rounded-2xl p-4 bg-surface-light/20">
          <div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-1">
            <h3 class="font-semibold text-sm">{svc.name}</h3>
            <span class="font-mono-accent text-xs text-accent">{svc.port}</span>
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-text-muted">{svc.desc}</p>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Closing -->
  <section class="reveal">
    <div class="border border-surface-light rounded-2xl p-8 bg-brand/5">
      <h2 class="font-display text-2xl font-bold mb-3">What this actually says about how I work</h2>
      <p class="text-text-muted leading-relaxed mb-5">
        Operating under a hard constraint. Evaluating real trade-offs instead of reaching for the
        buzzword. Catching a security issue in my <em>own</em> work &mdash; a live API key leaked in
        client-side JavaScript &mdash; and fixing it the same day. And being willing to reverse a
        decision out loud instead of quietly walking it back. That's the engineer I am.
      </p>
      <a href="https://github.com/iamvanshkhanna/homelab-ops-mesh" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-6 py-3 rounded-full bg-cta text-white font-semibold hover:bg-cta-hover transition-colors">
        Read the real commit history &rarr;
      </a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
